+# Cancellation
+
+For whoever changes cancellation or answers a member who missed the cutoff. A member can cancel a pantry order
+through the app only before packing starts. Late cancellations had left packed bags of fresh food unused; the
+cutoff protects that food and the volunteers' packing work.
+
+## Cutoff
+
+[`can_cancel(pickup_starts, now)`](../../larder/cancellation.py) returns whether cancellation is still allowed.
+`CUTOFF` is two hours, and the check is strictly `now < pickup_starts - CUTOFF`: cancellation is refused at the
+cutoff itself and afterward.
+
+For a pickup slot starting at 17:00, cancellation is allowed before 15:00. At 15:00, it is already refused.
+
+## Why packing sets the limit
+
+[Issue 31](../../notes/issue-31.md) records three Saturdays in a row with packed bags left on the shelf. On the
+latest Saturday, nine bags contained milk, bread and greens; members were cancelling at 16:50 for a 17:00 pickup.
+By October 7, the pantry had thrown away 25 bags of fresh food that month.
+
+A reminder banner had been in the app since summer and had not prevented these cancellations. The packing shift
+starts two hours before each pickup slot: the pantry reported that cancellations before packing cost nothing,
+while cancellations afterward waste a packed bag. The cutoff therefore follows the start of packing.
+
+## After the cutoff
+
+The agreement in issue 31 is that a member who cannot come after the cutoff calls the pantry, and the volunteers
+decide what to do. The pantry lead required the pantry phone number in the refusal message.
+
+The repository implements the eligibility check only; it contains neither a refusal message nor the pantry phone
+number. The phone fallback is the recorded agreement, and its presentation in the app cannot be verified here.
